gnore\\n.gitkeep\\n.gitpod.Dockerfile\\n.gitpod.yml\\n.jscpd.json\\n.lenv.example\\n.lycheeignore\\n.npmrc\\n.prettierignore\\n.prettierrc\\n.secretlintignore\\n.secretlintrc.json\\nanalyze-issue.md\\nCHANGELOG.md\\nclaude-runtime.mjs\\ncleanup-test-repos.mjs\\ncreate-test-repo.mjs\\ncspell.json\\ndo.mjs\\ndocker-compose.yml\\ndocker-restore-auth.sh\\ndocker-solve.sh\\nDockerfile\\nDockerfile.dind\\nDockerfile.e2e\\nDockerfile.formal-ai\\neslint.config.mjs\\ngitpod-backup-auth.sh\\ngitpod-restore-auth.sh\\nLICENSE\\nmain\\nmain.rs\\npackage-lock.json\\npackage.json\\nREADME.hi.md\\nREADME.md\\nREADME.ru.md\\nREADME.zh.md\\n</files>\"},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"You are an AI issue solver using @link-assistant/agent. Prefer minimal local CPU, memory, and disk use during development.\\nGeneral guidelines.\\n   - When you execute commands and the output becomes large, save the logs to files for easier review.\\n   - When running commands, avoid setting a timeout yourself. Let them run as long as needed.\\n   - When running sudo commands, especially package installations, run them in the background to avoid timeout issues.\\n   - When CI is failing, download the logs locally and investigate them carefully.\\n   - When a code or log file has more than 1500 lines, read it in chunks of 1500 lines.\\n   - When diagnosing a complex problem, prefer targeted tracing and enable verbose output only where useful.\\n   - When you create debug, test, or example scripts while fixing an issue, keep them in ./examples and/or ./experiments so you can reuse them later.\\n   - When you test assumptions, keep experiment scripts in ./experiments.\\n   - When an experiment demonstrates a real-world use case of the software, add it to ./examples.\\nInitial research.\\n   - When you start, outline the work and track requirements and verification in your plan.\\n   - When you read issue, read all details and comments thoroughly.\\n   - When you see screenshots or images in issue descriptions, pull request descriptions, comments, or discussions, download the image to a local file first, then use the Read tool to view and analyze it. Before reading downloaded images with the Read tool, verify that the file is a valid image rather than HTML by using a CLI tool such as the 'file' command. When the file command shows \\\"HTML\\\", \\\"text\\\", or \\\"ASCII text\\\", the download failed, so do not call Read on that file. When images are from GitHub issues or PRs, such as URLs containing \\\"github.com/user-attachments\\\", use: curl -L -H \\\"Authorization: [REDACTED] $(gh auth token)\\\" -o <filename> \\\"<url>\\\"\\n   - When you need issue details, use gh issue view https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4031.\\n   - When you need related code, use gh search code --owner link-assistant [keywords].\\n   - When you need repo context, read files in your working directory.\\n   - When you study related work, study the most recent related pull requests.\\n   - When the issue is not defined clearly enough, write a comment with clarifying questions.\\n   - When accessing GitHub Gists, use gh gist view command instead of direct URL fetching.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug, find the actual root cause first and run as many experiments as needed.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug and the code does not have enough tracing or logs, add them and keep them in the code with the default state switched off.\\n   - When you need comments on a pull request, note that GitHub has three different comment types with different API endpoints:\\n      1. PR review comments (inline code comments): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4037/comments --paginate\\n      2. PR conversation comments (general discussion): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4037/comments --paginate\\n      3. PR reviews (approve/request changes): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4037/reviews --paginate\\n      Note: The command \\\"gh pr view --json comments\\\" only returns conversation comments and misses review comments.\\n   - When you need the latest comments on the issue, use gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4031/comments --paginate.\\nSolution development and testing.\\n   - When coding, commit each atomic step that is useful on its own to the pull request branch so interrupted work remains preserved in the pull request.\\n   - When implementing changes, prefer drafting code for all requirements before starting verification, then use focused checks and existing CI/CD coverage.\\n   - When validating changes, prefer a single relevant test or small focused checks; check whether the command triggers a full source build before running it.\\n   - When CI/CD already covers full test suites or CPU-intensive builds, prefer leaving those to CI/CD and checking results for the current commit; avoid duplicating them locally unless user requirements or uncovered failures call for it.\\n   - When CI/CD coverage is missing and adding it was not requested, suggest https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/main/docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md; let the repository owner decide whether to add it, and meanwhile complete required validation with available resources.\\n   - When writing tests, prefer small cases with mocks and the existing integration framework.\\n   - When you test solution draft, include automated checks in pr.\\n   - When you write or modify tests, consider setting reasonable timeouts at test, suite, and CI job levels so failures surface quickly instead of hanging.\\n   - When you see repeated test timeout patterns in CI, investigate the root cause rather than increasing timeouts.\\n   - When the issue is unclear, write a comment on the issue with questions.\\n   - When you encounter any problems that you are unable to solve yourself, write a comment to the pull request asking for help.\\n   - When you need human help, use gh pr comment 4037 --body \\\"your message\\\" to comment on existing PR.\\nReproducible testing.\\n   - When fixing a bug, prefer a minimal regression test that fails before the fix and passes afterward.\\n   - When encountering UI bugs, capture a screenshot showing the problem state, then create a visual regression test or manual verification screenshot after the fix.\\n   - When submitting a fix, include in the PR description: (1) how to reproduce the issue, (2) the automated test that verifies the fix, (3) before/after screenshots for UI issues.\\n   - When a bug fix does not have a reproducing test, treat the fix as incomplete because regressions can occur later without notice.\\nPreparing pull request.\\n   - When you code, follow contributing guidelines.\\n   - When you commit, write clear message.\\n   - When you need examples of style, use gh pr list --repo link-assistant/hive-mind --state merged --search [keywords].\\n   - When you open pr, describe solution draft and include tests.\\n   - When there is a package with version and GitHub Actions workflows for automatic release, update the version in your pull request to prepare for next release.\\n   - When you update existing pr 4037, use gh pr edit to modify title and description.\\n   - When you finalize the pull request:\\n      check that the pull request title and description are updated (the PR may start with a [WIP] prefix and a placeholder description that should be replaced with the actual title and description of the changes),\\n      follow style from merged prs for code, title, and description,\\n      check that no uncommitted changes corresponding to the original requirements are left behind,\\n      check that the default branch is merged into the pull request branch,\\n      check that all CI checks are passing if they exist before you finish, including checks that already fail on the default branch (when a check needs a human action, make CI on the pull request pass without it and CI on the default branch fail with an explicit message saying what to do),\\n      check for latest comments on the issue and pull request to ensure no recent feedback was missed,\\n      double-check that all changes in the pull request address the original requirements of the issue,\\n      check for newly introduced bugs in the pull request by carefully reading gh pr diff,\\n      check that no previously existing features were removed without an explicit request in the issue description, issue comments, or pull request comments.\\n   - When you finish implementation, use gh pr ready 4037.\\nWorkflow and collaboration.\\n   - When you check branch, verify with git branch --show-current.\\n   - When you push, push only to branch issue-4031-3b456b148c52.\\n   - When you finish, create a pull request from branch issue-4031-3b456b148c52.\\n   - When pr 4037 already exists for this branch, update it instead of creating new one.\\n   - When you organize workflow, use pull requests instead of direct merges to default branch (main or master).\\n   - When you manage commits, preserve commit history for later analysis.\\n   - When you contribute, keep repository history forward-moving with regular commits, pushes, and reverts if needed.\\n   - When you face conflict that you cannot resolve yourself, ask for help.\\n   - When you collaborate, respect branch protections by working only on issue-4031-3b456b148c52.\\n   - When you mention a result, include the pull request URL or comment URL.\\nSelf review.\\n   - When you check your solution draft, verify git status shows a clean working tree with no uncommitted changes.\\n   - When you compare with repo style, use gh pr diff [number].\\n   - When you finalize, confirm code, tests, and description are consistent.\\nGitHub CLI command patterns.\\n   - When fetching lists from GitHub API, use the --paginate flag to ensure all results are returned (GitHub returns max 30 per page by default).\\n   - When adding PR comment, use gh pr comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When adding issue comment, use gh issue comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When viewing PR details, use gh pr view NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When filtering with jq, use gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4037/comments --paginate --jq 'reverse | .[0:5]'.\\nPlaywright MCP usage (browser automation via MCP tools).\\n   - When you develop frontend web applications (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Vue, Angular, etc.), use Playwright MCP tools to test the UI in a real browser.\\n   - When WebFetch tool fails to retrieve expected content (e.g., returns empty content, JavaScript-rendered pages, or login-protected pages), use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for web browsing.\\n   - When WebSearch tool fails or returns insufficient results, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for internet search.\\n   - When you need to interact with dynamic web pages that require JavaScript execution, use Playwright MCP tools.\\n   - When you need to visually verify how a web page looks or take screenshots, use browser_take_screenshot from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you need to fill forms, click buttons, or perform user interactions on web pages, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_click, browser_type, browser_fill_form).\\n   - When you need to test responsive design or different viewport sizes, use browser_resize from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you finish using the browser, close it with browser_close to free resources.\\n   - When reproducing UI bugs, use browser_take_screenshot to capture the problem state before implementing any fix.\\n   - When fixing UI bugs, take before/after screenshots to provide visual evidence of the fix for human verification.\\n   - When creating UI tests, save baseline screenshots to the repository for visual regression testing.\\n   - When verifying UI fixes, compare screenshots to ensure the fix does not introduce unintended visual changes.\\nIssue completion goal.\\n   - Do not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\n   - Try to set this exact objective with a native goal-setting API or tool before working, and keep it active until every acceptance criterion is verified. Codex exposes thread/goal/set through its app-server API when available; do not invent an exec --goal flag. If the running tool provides no native goal API, keep the objective and checklist in your work plan across turns and compaction.\\n   - Read the complete issue, every comment, every required sub-issue, and all pull request conversation comments, inline comments, and reviews. Inventory each requirement, including prose requirements and later feedback, and map each one to implementation and verification evidence.\\n   - Passing CI, a partial implementation, an upstream report, or a workaround does not complete an unmet acceptance criterion. Continue authorized work. If an external blocker cannot be resolved, report the exact blocked criteria and evidence, leave them unfinished, and keep the pull request unmerged.\\n   - Preserve a positive closing keyword in the pull request description for the original issue and every required issue. Repeat the keyword per issue, use the correct repository for foreign issues, and keep references outside examples or code blocks. A negated sentence (for example \\\"does not close\\\" before an issue reference) is not a closing reference. Mention issues you reported to other repositories without a keyword: GitHub also closes an issue linked right after \\\"fix:\\\", as in \\\"a suggested fix: <issue URL>\\\".\\nIssue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4031\\nYour prepared branch: issue-4031-3b456b148c52\\nYour prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794308204637\\nYour prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/4037\\nCollect data related about the issue to this repository, make sure we compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/4031/pulls/4037 folder.\\nDo not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\nProceed.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me run the requested command to get that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_e939dd8970a32b70\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4031' --json title --jq .title && echo && gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4031' --json body --jq .body; printf '\\\\n__formal_ai_exit=%s\\\\n' $?\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_e939dd8970a32b70\",\"content\":\"Auto-restart on uncommitted changes never applies its \\\"No progress\\\" stop when an old session-kill notice is counted as a new comment\\n\\n## What happens\\n\\nRun: `solve <issue-url> --model formal-ai --tool codex --attach-logs --verbose --no-tool-check --disable-report-issue --language en`, solve v2.39.10, 2026-11-10, pull request https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/pull/2. The provider is only the one used in the evidence run; nothing below depends on the model or the tool.\\n\\nLog (2837 lines): https://gist.githubusercontent.com/konard/f0e689ac80237a960f865873b14f38b8/raw/1eae4c63babdd5783808d7c0f71330c3919dea2c/tmp-hive-mind-log-upload-usQxob-sanitized.log.txt (quotes below omit the timestamp prefix and the leading emoji).\\n\\n1. The primary session ends with uncommitted changes and the temporary watch mode starts (line 708 `AUTO-RESTART: Uncommitted changes detected`, line 722 `Max iterations:         5`). The uncommitted set is the same on every check (lines 735-739): `M tests/verify-output.sh`, `?? .github/`.\\n2. Every restart ends with the same verdict, and the next restart starts anyway:\\n\\n   | Restart | \\\"No progress: this session ended exactly like the previous one\\\" | Next restart starts |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | 1/5 (line 741) | line 1095 | line 1169 `Restart 2/5` |\\n   | 2/5 | line 1523 | line 1597 `Restart 3/5` |\\n   | 3/5 | line 1951 | line 2025 `Restart 4/5` |\\n   | 4/5 | line 2379 | line 2453 `Restart 5/5` |\\n   | 5/5 | line 2807 | budget exhausted |\\n\\n3. Each of the five restarts used 164,265 input and 28,942 output tokens (lines 951, 1379, 1807, 2235, 2663): 821,325 input and 144,710 output tokens for five sessions the solver itself had classified as identical. Wall time from `Restart 1/5` (09:14:10) to the end of restart 5 (09:22:43): 8.5 minutes.\\n4. The run ends with https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/pull/2#issuecomment-6763712572 (\\\"Auto-restart 5/5 - limit reached\\\", \\\"Remaining blocker: uncommitted changes remained: M tests/verify-output.sh, ?? .github/\\\").\\n\\nFor comparison, the CI-failure loop of the same version stops after one repeat. Scala run, https://gist.githubusercontent.com/konard/a32d7924514dd15c26dde56c8824e096/raw/70f75187bbf431206f983668c6d105bfb6d6de47/tmp-hive-mind-log-upload-22ViXH-sanitized.log.txt:\\n\\n- line 29329: `[session-progress] auto-restart-until-mergeable: repeated=true inputChanged=true occurrences=2 ...`\\n- line 29331: `NO PROGRESS BETWEEN SESSIONS Stopping instead of repeating an identical session`\\n- line 29334: `Budget left unused:     4 restart iterations`\\n\\nThe Rust log contains no `[session-progress]` line at all: the guard was never evaluated there.\\n\\n## Expected\\n\\nA restart loop stops as soon as a session repeats the previous one, whatever started the loop. A comment that Hive Mind itself posted three weeks earlier does not count as new human feedback, and one comment cannot switch the guard off for every iteration of a run.\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\nCode read at 2.39.14 (commit b245deb62fbbca42625753587575416c50d81727). The evidence run is 2.39.10; whether the cited lines are identical in 2.39.10 was not verified.\\n\\nThree things combine.\\n\\n1. The repeat guard is skipped whenever any new comment is counted. [`src/solve.watch.lib.mjs` lines 341-343](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/b245deb62fbbca42625753587575416c50d81727/src/solve.watch.lib.mjs#L341-L343):\\n\\n   ```js\\n   const hasNewHumanComments = newPrComments + newPrReviewComments + newIssueComments > 0;\\n   noProgressStop = hasNewHumanComments ? null : await stopWhenSessionRepeated({ ... mode: 'watch' ... });\\n   if (noProgressStop) break;\\n   ```\\n\\n   `src/solve.restart-shared.lib.mjs` lines 546-549 only print the \\\"No progress\\\" line; they stop nothing.\\n\\n2. Every feedback check in the run reports one new comment (lines 161-164, 731-734, 1157-1160, 1585-1588, 2013-2016, 2441-2444):\\n\\n   ```\\n   Last commit time:       2026-10-20T22:35:56.000Z\\n   New PR comments:        1\\n   New PR review comments: 0\\n   New issue comments:     0\\n   ```\\n\\n   The only comment that is newer than that commit and is not filtered is https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/pull/2#issuecomment-6303319315, created 2026-10-20T22:36:24Z. It is Hive Mind's own session-kill notice; its first line is `<!-- hive-mind:session-kill-notice -->`. That marker (`KILL_RECOVERY_NOTICE_MARKER`, `src/session-kill-recovery.lib.mjs` line 33) is not in `TOOL_GENERATED_COMMENT_MARKERS` ([`src/tool-comments.lib.mjs` lines 152-154](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/b245deb62fbbca42625753587575416c50d81727/src/tool-comments.lib.mjs#L152-L154)), so `isToolGeneratedComment` (lines 176-179) returns false, and no `logPatterns` entry in `src/solve.feedback.lib.mjs` (line 104) matches it either. `isToolStatusComment` (lines 194-199) would accept the `<!-- hive-mind:` prefix, but the feedback counter does not use it.\\n\\n   Checked by matching the comment body text against the 34 markers and the log patterns of the source (0 hits). The neighbouring comment 6303318912 (\\\"Intermediate working-session log (killed session)\\\") has hits and is filtered. The source was not executed.\\n\\n3. The counting window is \\\"since the last commit\\\" (`src/solve.feedback.lib.mjs` line 74). A loop whose problem is that nothing gets committed never moves that window, so the same comment is counted again on every iteration.\\n\\nResult: `hasNewHumanComments` is true for all five restarts and `stopWhenSessionRepeated` is never called.\\n\\n## Suggested fix\\n\\n- Treat every comment that starts with a `<!-- hive-mind:` marker as tool-generated in `isToolGeneratedComment`, or at least add `KILL_RECOVERY_NOTICE_MARKER` to `TOOL_GENERATED_COMMENT_MARKERS`.\\n- Add a test that every marker constant the solver writes into a comment is recognised by `isToolGeneratedComment`, so a new comment type cannot be forgotten again.\\n- Inside a restart loop, count a comment as new only once: remember the comment ids already seen in this run (or use the start of the previous session as the window when no commit was made). The repeat guard is then skipped at most for the one iteration that follows real feedback.\\n- Keep the guard itself identical in both loops (see #1355).\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n- Rust log lines 708, 721-723, 735-739, 741, 1095, 1169, 1523, 1597, 1951, 2025, 2379, 2453, 2807 (URL above); token lines 951, 1379, 1807, 2235, 2663.\\n- Feedback counts: lines 161-164, 731-734, 1157-1160, 1585-1588, 2013-2016, 2441-2444.\\n- Stale comment: https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/pull/2#issuecomment-6303319315\\n- Final comment: https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/pull/2#issuecomment-6763712572\\n- Scala log lines 29329, 29331, 29334 (URL above).\\n\\n## Related issues\\n\\n- Related: #1355 (one restart engine for all restart reasons; state: open).\\n- Related: #2839 and #2313 (earlier reports about the no-progress breaker; state of both: not open).\\n- Related: #3435 (another defect of the session-kill notice; state: open).\\n- Related: #4032 (what happens after the budget is exhausted in this run).\\n\\nReported from the analysis in https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1548.\\n\\n\\n__formal_ai_exit=0\\n\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me run the requested command to get that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_47129670f03df908\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 && python3 -c 'import json\\\\nimport os\\\\nimport pathlib\\\\nimport secrets\\\\nimport stat\\\\nimport sys\\\\n\\\\nMAXIMUM_FILE_BYTES = 16 * 1024 * 1024\\\\nplan = json.loads(sys.argv[1])\\\\nroot = pathlib.Path.cwd()\\\\n\\\\n\\\\ndef owned_path(value):\\\\n    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value or '\\\\\\\\''\\\\\\\\x00'\\\\\\\\'' in value:\\\\n        raise ValueError('\\\\\\\\''invalid_owned_path'\\\\\\\\'')\\\\n    relative = pathlib.PurePosixPath(value)\\\\n    if relative.is_absolute() or pathlib.PureWindowsPath(value).drive or '\\\\\\\\''..'\\\\\\\\'' in relative.parts or str(relative) != value:\\\\n        raise ValueError('\\\\\\\\''invalid_owned_path'\\\\\\\\'')\\\\n    current = root\\\\n    for part in relative.parts:\\\\n        current = current / part\\\\n        if current.is_symlink():\\\\n            raise ValueError('\\\\\\\\''symbolic_owned_path'\\\\\\\\'')\\\\n    return current\\\\n\\\\n\\\\ndef read_regular(path, limit):\\\\n    descriptor = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | getattr(os, '\\\\\\\\''O_NOFOLLOW'